// NEGATIVE TEST — a range pattern must be able to match (N6-03).
//
// `5..=1` is empty: no integer is both >= 5 and <= 1. The arm it heads can never
// be taken, so the program a reader thought they wrote is not the program that
// would run — and the two numbers are almost always a transposition. Compiling
// it into `x >= 5 && x <= 1` and letting the arm silently never fire is how a
// typo becomes a behaviour change nobody sees.
//
// `3..3` is the same defect in the exclusive form: `>= 3 && < 3`.
fn main() {
    let v = match 4 {
        5..=1 => 1,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
